---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CTABlock from '../components/CTABlock.astro';
import Arch from '../components/Arch.astro';
import SectionDivider from '../components/SectionDivider.astro';
import { STAGE_DETAIL } from '../lib/method-page';
import { FAQS } from '../lib/faq';
import { breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema } from '../lib/schema';

const path = '/method';

const schema = [
  breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Method', path },
  ]),
  faqSchema(
    FAQS.map((faq) => ({ question: faq.question, answer: faq.answer })),
    path
  ),
];
---

<Base
  title="The Sonora Method — Content and Search Strategy Framework"
  description="A four-stage sequence for becoming the answer in AI and traditional search: Listen, Locate, Saturate, Sustain."
  path={path}
  schema={schema}
>
  <!-- 1. Hero -->
  <section class="section page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose">
        <h1>The Sonora Method</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          A sequence, not a playbook. Four stages, in order, every time. The
          stages never change. What they produce never repeats.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. The loud room problem -->
  <section class="section surface-ink" aria-labelledby="loud-room">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose" data-reveal>
        <h2 id="loud-room">The loud room problem</h2>
        <p>
          Every company in every category publishes now. The tooling got cheap,
          then it got free, and the volume went up to match. The standard
          response to a crowded channel has been to add more of everything, on
          the theory that presence is a numbers game.
        </p>
        <p>
          It stopped being one a while ago. The room is loud enough that volume
          is indistinguishable from noise, and buyers have better filters than
          they used to.
        </p>
        <p>
          What they do instead is narrow. They ask one specific question and they
          want one good answer. Increasingly they ask a model, and the model
          returns a synthesis with a handful of citations attached. There is no
          page two. There is often no page one.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is bad for anyone competing on output. It is very good for anyone
          who can be the definitive answer to a narrow question.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. Why playbooks don't work here -->
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="playbooks">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose" data-reveal>
        <h2 id="playbooks">Why playbooks don&rsquo;t work here</h2>
        <p>
          A playbook is a set of decisions made in advance by someone who has not
          seen your data. It travels well and it applies badly. It survives
          because it can be sold repeatedly with the client&rsquo;s logo swapped
          in.
        </p>
        <p>
          Answer engine optimization, or AEO &mdash; the practice of getting a
          company cited inside AI-generated answers rather than ranked in a list
          of links &mdash; is a poor fit for that model. The work sits downstream
          of specifics. Which questions your buyers ask in their own words. Which
          sources a model pulls from when it answers them. Whether your site is
          legible to a crawler at all. Which competitor already owns the sentence
          you want. None of that is knowable in advance, and every one of them
          changes what you should do first.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the method is a sequence. The order is the part that transfers. The
          content of each stage is yours.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. The four stages, expanded -->
  {
    STAGE_DETAIL.map((stage, index) => (
      <section
        class:list={['section stage', index % 2 === 0 ? 'surface-ink' : '']}
        aria-labelledby={`stage-${stage.number}`}
      >
        <div class="container">
          <div class="stage-inner" data-reveal>
            <div class="numeral">
              <Arch variant="outline" label={stage.number} size={96} />
            </div>
            <div class="prose">
              <h2 id={`stage-${stage.number}`}>
                {stage.number} &middot; {stage.name}
              </h2>
              {stage.paragraphs.map((paragraph) => (
                <p>{paragraph}</p>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <SectionDivider />

  <!-- 5. Categories don't create themselves -->
  <section class="section categories" aria-labelledby="categories">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose" data-reveal>
        <h2 id="categories">Categories don&rsquo;t create themselves</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          The most expensive mistake in this work is a company deciding it has
          created a category because it has started using a phrase.
        </p>
        <p>
          A category exists when other people use the word without being
          prompted. Not your marketing site. Analysts, competitors, buyers
          describing their own problem, job listings, and the models that have
          read all of it. Until then you have a tagline, and a tagline is a claim
          you are making about yourself.
        </p>
        <p>
          The mechanism is worth being precise about, because it explains why
          this is slow. A model&rsquo;s answer about your category is assembled
          from what it has read. If the only places using your term are
          properties you own, the term reads as proprietary language and gets
          translated back into the category the rest of the market uses. You get
          described as a version of the incumbent. That description then feeds
          the next answer, and the answer after that.
        </p>
        <p>
          Getting out of that loop requires the term to appear in sources you do
          not own, attached to a definition that stays consistent everywhere it
          appears. A definition short enough to be quoted. Published somewhere
          quotable. Repeated without being reworded every time somebody prefers a
          different adjective. It requires research that gives other people a
          reason to cite the term while citing the data. And it requires the term
          to survive contact with sales calls, where the buyer either recognises
          the problem it names or does not.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most companies should not attempt it. The correct move for the majority
          is to win a narrow existing question decisively, which is faster and
          more defensible. Naming a category is right when the existing category
          actively misdescribes what you do, and the cost of being misdescribed
          is larger than the cost of the campaign.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is a strategy decision and it should be made deliberately, early,
          and with someone willing to argue the other side. It is the single most
          common thing I am hired to argue about.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SectionDivider />

  <!-- 6. What an engagement looks like -->
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="engagement">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose" data-reveal>
        <h2 id="engagement">What an engagement looks like</h2>
        <p>
          Most engagements start with an audit, because I am not going to tell
          you what to do before I know what is true. The audit runs the first two
          stages: the query set, the AI visibility baseline, the technical
          teardown, the competitive picture, and a position with a sequenced
          roadmap attached.
        </p>
        <p>
          After that the shape depends on what you already have. If you have
          people producing work but nobody setting direction, the engagement is
          standing direction: strategy, editorial standard, quarterly
          measurement, and management of whoever executes. If you have a plan and
          development capacity but nobody to bridge them, the engagement is
          translating the roadmap into tickets an engineering team will
          prioritise, then verifying that what shipped did what it was supposed
          to.
        </p>
        <p>
          I set strategy and direction. I do not write your posts or build your
          site. Your team or your contractors do that, and my job is deciding
          what should be made, setting the standard it has to meet, and holding
          the line on whether it got met.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first thing I will usually tell you is something you did not want
          to hear about your positioning. That is the value. Everything after it
          is sequencing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="section surface-ink faq" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose" data-reveal>
        <h2 id="faq-heading">Common questions</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
          {
            FAQS.map((faq) => (
              <div class="faq-item">
                <h3>{faq.question}</h3>
                <p>{faq.answer}</p>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABlock />
</Base>

<style>
  .page-hero {
    padding-block: clamp(56px, 7vw, 96px) clamp(48px, 6vw, 72px);
  }

  .page-hero .lead {
    margin-top: 28px;
  }

  .stage-inner {
    display: grid;
    gap: 24px;
  }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .stage-inner {
      grid-template-columns: 120px 1fr;
      gap: 56px;
    }
  }

  .numeral {
    line-height: 0;
  }

  .categories {
    padding-block: clamp(88px, 11vw, 160px);
  }

  .categories .lead {
    margin-top: 24px;
  }

  .faq-list {
    margin-top: 40px;
  }

  .faq-item + .faq-item {
    margin-top: 40px;
    padding-top: 40px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--surface-rule);
  }

  .faq-item h3 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
  }

  .faq-item p {
    margin-top: 12px;
  }
</style>
